    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jose Caballero** (80.26) anchors our lineup as the highest-scoring play, buoyed by a significant +13% platoon advantage against Lopez and a favorable park factor. While **Corbin Carroll** (73.67) and **James Wood** (71.32) trail slightly, they remain elite options; **James Wood** specifically benefits from an leadoff spot and a soft matchup against Canning, while **Corbin Carroll** retains his status as a must-start due to his elite sprint speed, which is critical for our pursuit of the stolen base category. **Brice Turang** (71.96) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.46) also project as high-floor cornerstones, as both leverage the +7% SP skill advantage against their respective right-handed matchups.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Austin Riley** (51.65), who just missed the cut. While **Austin Riley** carries a strong pedigree, his matchup against the southpaw Lodolo and a negative SP skill adjustment dropped him behind our higher-efficiency starts. Our lineup composition remains aggressive regarding speed; even though **Cole Young** (45.59) carries a marginal score, his role as a stolen base threat against a slow-delivering Kelly kept him in the lineup over bench options like **Zach McKinstry** (45.24) and **Travis Bazzana** (42.63), both of whom suffered from poorer platoon splits. \n\nWe are monitoring the wind as a potential boost for **Dalton Rushing** (54.13) and **Alex Freeland** (49.29) in their matchup against Painter, though their low batting order positions keep them in the marginal tier. Be advised that while **Seiya Suzuki** (61.58) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (63.23) are solid starts, they represent our mid-tier confidence levels today. With **Mickey Moniak** currently sidelined on the IL, we are leaning heavily on the rest of the roster to capitalize on these specific pitching vulnerabilities; ensure all active players are confirmed in their respective lineups to avoid any late-scratch risks.",
